[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Amazon ECR Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Amazon ECR Purpose and Container Image Lifecycle",
        "content": "Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) is a managed container image registry in AWS. It stores, manages, and distributes container images and compatible artifacts used by services such as Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, AWS Fargate, and EC2-based container platforms. A typical lifecycle begins when a developer or CI pipeline builds an image, authenticates to ECR, tags the image with a repository reference, and pushes it to a registry. Deployment systems later authenticate and pull the approved image to run workloads. ECR removes the need for an organization to operate its own registry servers, while AWS manages the underlying service infrastructure. Teams remain responsible for repository design, access permissions, image governance, retention, and release traceability."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Private and Public ECR Registry Models",
        "content": "Amazon ECR provides private and public repository models for different distribution requirements. Private ECR repositories are designed for controlled access within AWS accounts and can be accessed by authorized identities and workloads. They are commonly used for internal application images, approved base images, and organization-specific build artifacts. Amazon ECR Public supports publishing images for broader distribution through public repositories and galleries. The choice affects authentication, visibility, access governance, and distribution strategy. A private repository should be the default for proprietary application images and internal dependencies. Public publication requires an explicit review of image contents, licensing, secrets, metadata, and support expectations because published artifacts may be accessible to external consumers."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "AWS Account, Region, and Registry Endpoint Identity",
        "content": "An Amazon ECR private registry is associated with an AWS account and an AWS Region. A private registry URI commonly follows the form ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com, where the account identifier and Region determine the registry endpoint. A repository name is appended to this endpoint to form a complete image reference. This structure matters in multi-account and multi-Region deployments because an image in one account or Region is not automatically the same resource as an image with a similar name elsewhere. Always verify the AWS account, Region, registry endpoint, and repository before pushing or pulling. Incorrect endpoint selection can cause authentication errors, accidental publication to the wrong account, or deployment of an unintended artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "ECR Repositories, Image Manifests, and Layers",
        "content": "An ECR repository stores image manifests and the content layers referenced by those manifests. A manifest describes image configuration and the layers required to assemble the image, while layers contain filesystem changes produced during image construction. Container clients use the registry API to retrieve the manifest and then download the referenced layers. Shared layer content can reduce repeated transfer and storage work when images have common base layers, subject to registry implementation and retention behavior. Understanding manifests and layers helps explain why an image is more than a single compressed file. It also helps operators investigate missing manifests, incomplete pushes, architecture mismatches, and unexpected storage usage. Use image metadata and digest information when tracing the exact artifact stored in ECR."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Supported Container Formats and Artifact Compatibility",
        "content": "Amazon ECR supports container image workflows based on Docker and OCI-compatible image formats. OCI image manifests and indexes provide standardized structures for describing image configuration and platform-specific content. This compatibility allows common container tooling to build, tag, push, inspect, and pull images using ECR as the registry endpoint. Teams should still validate the client version, image format, manifest type, and target runtime when introducing new build tools or multi-platform workflows. A successful push does not guarantee that every older runtime can consume every manifest format. Test the published artifact with the actual deployment platform and record the image digest and supported architectures as part of release verification."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "ECR Integration with Docker and Build Pipelines",
        "content": "Docker and CI tools interact with ECR through standard registry operations. A build pipeline first obtains AWS credentials through an approved identity mechanism, requests an ECR authorization token, and uses the returned credentials to authenticate its container client. It then tags the image with the complete ECR repository URI and pushes it. Build jobs should use narrowly scoped permissions and should not store long-lived AWS access keys in source code or pipeline YAML. Separate build permissions from deployment pull permissions wherever possible. Capture the source commit, pipeline execution, image tag, and digest so the artifact can be traced from source to deployment. This makes ECR a controlled artifact distribution point in a repeatable CI/CD process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "ECR Image Scanning and Supply-Chain Visibility",
        "content": "Container images may contain vulnerable operating-system packages, application dependencies, and bundled tools. Amazon ECR provides image scanning capabilities that can identify known vulnerabilities in supported image contents. Depending on the configured scanning mode and service capabilities, findings may be available when an image is pushed or through continuous scanning workflows. A scan result is evidence for risk assessment, not a guarantee that an image is secure. Teams should evaluate severity, exploitability, package usage, available fixes, and deployment exposure. Establish a remediation process that rebuilds images with patched dependencies and republishes approved artifacts. Connect scan findings to release governance so critical issues can be blocked or require documented approval before production deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "ECR Pricing, Storage, and Data Transfer Considerations",
        "content": "ECR operating cost depends on factors such as stored image data, data transfer, and the selected AWS Region and service configuration. Frequent CI builds can create many tagged images and retain layers that are no longer needed, increasing storage consumption over time. Cross-Region or internet data transfer may introduce additional cost depending on the traffic path and AWS pricing rules. Use repository lifecycle policies to manage eligible image retention, but protect release artifacts that are required for production rollback or compliance. Monitor repository growth and review build practices that generate unnecessary duplicate artifacts. For accurate budgeting, consult current AWS pricing for the specific Region, transfer pattern, and features in use rather than relying on a fixed estimate."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "ECR Operational Responsibilities and Service Boundaries",
        "content": "Amazon ECR is a managed AWS service, but adopting it does not remove all registry administration responsibilities. AWS operates the service infrastructure, while the customer configures repository access, identity policies, encryption choices, lifecycle rules, image scanning, replication, and operational monitoring. The application team remains responsible for producing trustworthy images and maintaining their dependencies. Platform teams should define repository naming, account and Region strategy, access boundaries, retention standards, and incident ownership. Production teams should know how to identify a deployed digest, retrieve a previous release, and respond when image pulls fail. A clear responsibility model prevents gaps between cloud administration, CI pipeline ownership, security governance, and application operations."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "ECR Registry Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "ECR Registry Endpoint Structure and Resource Hierarchy",
        "content": "Amazon ECR private registry architecture begins with an AWS account and Region-specific registry endpoint. Within the registry, repositories organize related container images, and each repository can contain multiple image tags and digests. A complete private image reference combines the registry endpoint, repository name, and tag or digest. This hierarchy is important when designing naming conventions and permissions because policies can be scoped to particular repositories or registry resources. Separate repositories can represent different applications, services, or trust boundaries. Avoid relying on ambiguous repository names across accounts and Regions. Document the canonical registry URI for each environment and ensure deployment configuration selects the intended account, Region, repository, and immutable artifact identifier."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Account and Region Boundaries in Multi-Environment Design",
        "content": "ECR resources are Regional, and private registry access is associated with AWS account boundaries. Organizations commonly separate development, testing, staging, and production workloads into different accounts or use a controlled shared-services account for approved images. A multi-account design can reduce accidental access between environments and support independent administrative ownership. A multi-Region design can improve image availability near workloads and support recovery objectives. These choices introduce requirements for cross-account policies, replication, identity configuration, and consistent naming. Define which account owns the source image, which accounts may consume it, and how approved digests move between Regions. Avoid assuming that a repository name in another account automatically grants access to the original repository."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Repository Boundaries and Naming Architecture",
        "content": "Repository design should reflect application ownership, release lifecycle, and security boundaries. A repository per application or independently governed component often makes permissions, retention, and audit responsibilities easier to understand. Use consistent naming conventions that identify the owning team or platform area without encoding temporary details that frequently change. Avoid creating a separate repository for every build number; tags are normally better suited to identifying versions within a repository. Repository names should be stable and predictable so CI pipelines and deployment manifests can refer to them consistently. Document naming rules and ownership metadata, and review repository sprawl periodically. Good repository boundaries simplify access reviews, lifecycle management, and investigation of unexpected image publication."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Registry API, Authentication Token, and Client Interaction",
        "content": "Docker-compatible clients communicate with ECR through registry APIs to authenticate, upload manifests, and transfer image layers. For private ECR, an AWS identity with the required permissions obtains an authorization token through the ECR API. The token is then used by the container client to authenticate to the registry endpoint. The authorization token is temporary and should be refreshed according to its validity period and the execution environment's authentication workflow. AWS API authorization and registry authentication are related but distinct steps: the caller must be allowed to obtain the token, and the resulting identity must also have the required repository actions. Troubleshooting should identify whether failure occurs during AWS credential resolution, token retrieval, registry login, or image transfer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "IAM Policy Evaluation for Registry Operations",
        "content": "ECR access is governed through AWS identity and resource policies. Identity policies grant actions to IAM users, roles, or federated identities, while repository policies can grant access to specified principals and accounts. Effective access may also be affected by permission boundaries, service control policies, session policies, and explicit denies. A push workflow typically needs permission to obtain an authorization token and perform the required layer upload and image publication operations. A pull workflow needs the corresponding download and image retrieval permissions. Do not solve an access failure by granting broad administrator privileges. Identify the denied action from the error or audit evidence and add the narrowest required permission to the intended principal and repository scope."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Image Manifest and Layer Distribution Architecture",
        "content": "An ECR image is represented through a manifest or image index that references configuration metadata and content layers. During a push, the client checks which layers need uploading, transfers missing content, and publishes the manifest that connects those layers into an image reference. During a pull, the client retrieves the manifest and downloads the required layers. This model allows images to share common layers and supports efficient distribution. Multi-platform images use an index that references platform-specific manifests, allowing compatible clients to select the correct architecture. When investigating a missing image or platform error, inspect the repository manifest and digest rather than relying only on a tag name. This distinction helps isolate publishing problems from runtime compatibility problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Private Connectivity with VPC Endpoints",
        "content": "Workloads in private networks may need to access ECR without relying on public internet routing. AWS provides VPC endpoint options for ECR API and Docker registry access, and image layer transfer may also involve the relevant AWS storage service endpoint configuration. Correct endpoint setup depends on the network design, DNS behavior, security groups, route configuration, and the workload's Region. A missing or misconfigured endpoint can produce authentication or layer-download failures even when IAM permissions are correct. Validate endpoint policies and network reachability from the actual build agent or runtime host. Use private connectivity where it meets security and operational requirements, and document all required endpoints for each deployment Region."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Encryption, Logging, and Registry Governance Architecture",
        "content": "ECR repository architecture should include data protection and audit requirements. ECR supports encryption of stored image data, with repository configuration determining the applicable encryption arrangement and key-management integration. Organizations should evaluate key ownership, rotation responsibilities, access to encryption keys, and the operational impact of key policy changes. Audit and monitoring design should capture relevant AWS API activity, repository changes, image publication events, and access-policy modifications. CloudTrail can provide visibility into supported API activity, while monitoring and alerting can help identify unexpected operations. Define who can create repositories, change policies, publish images, and delete artifacts. Governance should be applied consistently across accounts and Regions rather than relying on individual teams to configure repositories independently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Replication and Availability Across Regions and Accounts",
        "content": "ECR replication can distribute images from a source registry to destination registries in supported accounts and Regions. This can reduce repeated manual pushes and improve image availability near deployment environments. Replication configuration must be designed around source and destination ownership, repository selection, permissions, and regional requirements. Replication is not a substitute for validating that the destination image is usable by its intended runtime identity. Confirm that expected repositories and image digests appear in destination registries and understand propagation timing. Include replication health and destination access in release verification. For disaster recovery, document how workloads switch to an alternate registry location and how the organization confirms that required production images are available there."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Reference Architecture for Secure CI/CD Image Distribution",
        "content": "A secure ECR reference architecture separates image creation, image storage, and image consumption. A CI role builds and tests an image, scans it, and pushes it to a controlled source repository. The pipeline records the resulting digest and source revision. Promotion workflows then make the approved artifact available to staging and production accounts through controlled cross-account access or registry replication. Runtime identities receive pull-only permissions and retrieve the approved digest. Repository policies, IAM roles, encryption settings, audit logging, lifecycle rules, and vulnerability governance enforce the platform standards. The design should also include private network access where required, recovery copies in appropriate Regions, and documented ownership. Validate the architecture with a complete push, promotion, pull, and rollback exercise before production adoption."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Repository Creation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "ECR Repository Purpose and Resource Model",
        "content": "An Amazon Elastic Container Registry repository is a logical location for storing and organizing container images. A repository belongs to an AWS account and Region and is addressed through a registry URI that includes the account identifier, regional ECR endpoint, and repository name. Images pushed to the repository are identified by tags and content digests. Repository boundaries help teams separate applications, environments, and ownership responsibilities. Before creating a repository, decide its naming convention, expected image volume, access model, encryption requirements, scanning policy, and retention needs. For example, a team may create separate repositories for an API, worker service, and frontend rather than placing every application image in one shared repository. A clear repository model improves IAM policy design, auditability, lifecycle management, and release traceability."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Creating Repositories with the AWS CLI",
        "content": "The AWS CLI can create an ECR repository using the create-repository operation. First confirm the active AWS identity with aws sts get-caller-identity and select the intended Region. Then run aws ecr create-repository with a repository name and Region, for example aws ecr create-repository --repository-name platform/orders-api --region ap-south-1. The response includes the repository ARN, URI, registry identifier, and creation metadata. These values should be captured by automation rather than manually reconstructed wherever possible. Repository creation requires the appropriate ECR control-plane permission, such as ecr:CreateRepository. A pipeline should handle the case where a repository already exists deliberately, instead of treating every creation error as success. Validate the resulting repository URI before using it in image tagging or push commands."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Repository Naming, Namespaces, and Team Organization",
        "content": "Repository names should communicate application ownership and purpose while remaining stable across releases. ECR supports repository names containing path-like namespace segments, which can organize repositories by team or product area, such as payments/transaction-api or platform/observability-agent. Define consistent rules for lowercase names, separators, environment boundaries, and application identifiers. Avoid encoding frequently changing deployment details into repository names when tags or release metadata are more suitable. Decide whether development and production images should use separate repositories or whether one immutable artifact should be promoted across environments. The choice affects access policies, lifecycle rules, scanning configuration, and operational ownership. Document repository naming conventions so developers and automation create predictable resources instead of introducing ad hoc names."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "Repository Configuration: Encryption and Image Scanning",
        "content": "ECR repositories support configuration choices that affect how stored images are protected and inspected. Repository encryption can use the supported default encryption behavior or a configured AWS KMS key, depending on the organization's security and compliance requirements. When a customer-managed KMS key is selected, the key policy and IAM permissions must allow the required ECR operations. Image scanning can be configured according to the scanning features and settings available in the account and Region. Teams should decide whether scan-on-push or continuous scanning behavior is required and how findings will be reviewed. Configure these controls as part of repository provisioning so newly created repositories do not depend on manual follow-up. Verify the effective repository settings after creation using the ECR describe-repositories operation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "Creating Repositories with Infrastructure as Code",
        "content": "Infrastructure as Code makes repository configuration repeatable and reviewable. In Terraform, an aws_ecr_repository resource can define the repository name, image mutability setting, encryption configuration, and image scanning configuration. CloudFormation provides an AWS::ECR::Repository resource for the same general purpose. Store repository definitions in version control and apply changes through a controlled deployment workflow. Use reusable modules when multiple services require consistent security and lifecycle settings, but expose only the options that teams genuinely need to configure. Review plan output before applying changes, particularly when changing encryption keys or repository names. Avoid creating the same repository through both manual CLI commands and infrastructure automation because unmanaged differences can cause drift and confusing ownership."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Repository Policies and Resource-Level Access",
        "content": "An ECR repository can have a repository policy that grants specified principals permission to perform supported repository operations. This is especially useful when another AWS account or external workload needs access to a particular repository. Repository policies complement identity-based IAM policies; the effective permission depends on the applicable policies, principal identity, and AWS authorization rules. Grant only the actions required by the consumer, such as image retrieval operations for a deployment system. Do not grant push or administrative permissions to consumers that only need to pull images. Review the policy using get-repository-policy and validate the principal account and role identifiers carefully. Repository policies should be version-controlled or otherwise audited so access changes can be traced and reviewed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "Repository Discovery, Inspection, and Configuration Verification",
        "content": "After creating a repository, verify that it exists in the intended AWS account and Region. Use aws ecr describe-repositories with the repository name and Region to inspect its URI, ARN, encryption configuration, image tag mutability, and scanning configuration where returned. Use list-images to inspect image identifiers and describe-images to retrieve metadata about specific tags or digests. Confirm that the repository belongs to the expected account by checking the registry identifier and active AWS identity. These checks are important when multiple AWS profiles, roles, and Regions are used by developers or CI agents. Treat a successful create-repository response as only the first step; configuration verification ensures that the actual resource matches the intended deployment design."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-8",
        "title": "Repository Provisioning Automation and Idempotency",
        "content": "A reliable provisioning script should be safe to execute repeatedly. Before creating a repository, automation can query whether the expected repository already exists and then compare its effective configuration with the desired configuration. If it is missing, create it; if it exists, report its current settings and apply only approved changes. Do not silently ignore access-denied, invalid-parameter, or wrong-Region errors as though the resource already existed. Capture the repository URI and ARN as outputs for later build and deployment stages. Keep account and Region selection explicit, and use a dedicated automation role with narrowly scoped permissions. Include validation for encryption, mutability, scanning, and repository policy so provisioning produces a known and reviewable state."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-3-section-9",
        "title": "Repository Deletion, Recovery Planning, and Operational Ownership",
        "content": "Deleting an ECR repository can permanently remove the images stored in it, so repository cleanup must be treated as a controlled operational change. The delete-repository operation normally requires the repository to be empty unless force deletion is explicitly requested. Before deletion, identify workloads, deployment manifests, rollback procedures, and other accounts that depend on the repository. Confirm that required images have been retained or copied to an approved destination and that recovery expectations are understood. Establish ownership for repository lifecycle, access reviews, vulnerability remediation, retention policy, and incident escalation. For production repositories, use change approval and audit logging before destructive operations. A repository should not be removed merely because its application is temporarily inactive or its latest deployment uses another image."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "AWS IAM Permissions",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "IAM Authorization Model for Amazon ECR",
        "content": "Amazon ECR authorization combines AWS identity-based permissions, repository resource policies, and the service-specific authorization behavior of the requested operation. IAM identities such as users and roles receive policies that allow or deny actions on ECR resources. Repository policies can grant access to principals that need to interact with a particular repository, including principals from another account. A successful Docker push or pull requires more than a valid AWS login: the identity must be authorized for the relevant ECR API operations and image-layer transfer workflow. Start permission analysis by identifying the caller identity, account, Region, registry, repository, and exact failed action. This avoids granting broad administrator access when only one repository permission is missing. Always evaluate explicit denies and applicable organizational controls as part of the investigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "Identity-Based Policies for ECR Operations",
        "content": "Identity-based IAM policies can be attached to users, groups, or roles to grant ECR permissions. For example, a build role may need permission to obtain an authorization token and upload image layers, while a deployment role may need permission to retrieve image manifests and layers. Use actions that match the actual workflow rather than granting every ECR action. The Resource element should be scoped to the relevant repository ARN when the action supports resource-level permissions. Some actions, including obtaining an ECR authorization token, require a wildcard resource because the permission is not scoped to an individual repository in the same way. Separate token authorization from repository-level push or pull permissions. Review AWS service authorization documentation when building policies because supported resource types and condition keys vary by action."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-3",
        "title": "ECR Authorization Token and GetAuthorizationToken Permission",
        "content": "Docker clients use an ECR authorization token to authenticate to a private registry. The AWS identity generally needs the ecr:GetAuthorizationToken permission to obtain this token. This action is commonly granted with Resource set to \"*\" because it is not restricted to one repository ARN in the policy statement. The token is temporary and is used by the Docker client for registry authentication; it does not independently grant unrestricted access to every repository. Actual image operations still require the relevant repository permissions. A common mistake is granting GetAuthorizationToken alone and expecting Docker push or pull to work. Build a policy with a separate statement for token retrieval and another appropriately scoped statement for the repository operations required by the workload."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-4",
        "title": "Least-Privilege Permissions for Image Push Workflows",
        "content": "An image publisher requires permissions for the ECR upload workflow. Common repository-level actions include ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, ecr:InitiateLayerUpload, ecr:UploadLayerPart, ecr:CompleteLayerUpload, and ecr:PutImage. The identity also needs ecr:GetAuthorizationToken to authenticate the Docker client. Scope repository-level actions to the specific repository ARN rather than every repository in the account whenever practical. A CI role that publishes one application's image should not automatically receive permission to overwrite unrelated service images. Consider whether the workflow also needs repository discovery or metadata inspection and grant those actions only when required. Test the policy with the real pipeline identity and review CloudTrail or error details when authorization fails. Keep publisher permissions separate from deployment-only pull permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-5",
        "title": "Least-Privilege Permissions for Image Pull Workflows",
        "content": "A workload that retrieves an image from ECR should receive read-only permissions appropriate to the pull workflow. Common repository actions include ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, ecr:BatchGetImage, and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer, together with ecr:GetAuthorizationToken. Scope repository access to the image repository or repositories required by the workload. Do not grant ecr:PutImage or layer-upload permissions to a runtime identity that only downloads images. Depending on the deployment platform, the permissions may be attached to an EC2 instance role, task execution role, node role, or another supported workload identity. Verify which identity actually performs the image pull; it may differ from the application process identity. This distinction is important when debugging a deployment that cannot retrieve a private image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-6",
        "title": "IAM Roles for EC2, ECS, EKS, and CI Workloads",
        "content": "ECR access should be granted to the AWS identity used by the component performing the registry operation. An EC2-based build agent may use an instance profile role, while an ECS task commonly relies on its task execution role for image retrieval. EKS image pulls from private ECR repositories are generally performed by the node's container runtime using the node's AWS permissions, subject to the cluster configuration and platform features in use. A CI system may assume a dedicated role through federation or an approved credentials provider. Do not assume that the application container's own role is automatically the identity used to pull its image before startup. Identify the exact principal, inspect its attached policies, and test the required ECR operation from the same execution context."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-7",
        "title": "Repository Resource Policies and Cross-Account Authorization",
        "content": "A repository policy can allow a principal in another AWS account to access a specific ECR repository. The consuming account may also need an identity-based policy that permits the required ECR actions. Cross-account access therefore requires coordinated authorization on both sides, with the repository owner granting access and the consumer identity being allowed to use it. For image pulls, grant only the required read operations; for publishing, add write operations only when the external publisher is trusted and expected to push. Validate the account ID, role ARN, repository ARN, and Region. Use get-repository-policy to inspect the effective repository policy and review CloudTrail records when access is denied. Avoid wildcard principals unless a carefully designed condition restricts the intended access."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-8",
        "title": "IAM Policy Conditions, Boundaries, and Explicit Denies",
        "content": "IAM authorization may be affected by conditions, permissions boundaries, session policies, service control policies, and explicit deny statements. A policy that appears to allow an ECR action may still be ineffective when another applicable policy denies it or limits the role's maximum permissions. Conditions can restrict access using supported context keys, such as principal or network-related attributes where applicable to the service and action. Do not add condition keys without verifying that ECR supplies them for the operation being evaluated. When troubleshooting, inspect the full role policy set, boundary, assumed-role session, and organization-level controls. Use IAM policy simulation where appropriate, then confirm behavior with a controlled request using the same principal. This method is more reliable than repeatedly adding broad permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-9",
        "title": "Diagnosing ECR AccessDenied Errors with AWS Evidence",
        "content": "An AccessDenied error should be analyzed using the failed operation and caller identity. Run aws sts get-caller-identity in the same shell or CI job that performs the ECR operation to confirm the active account and assumed role. Capture the Region, repository ARN, command, timestamp, and exact AWS error message without exposing credentials. Check whether the required action is allowed by identity policies and repository policies, and then review permissions boundaries and organization-level restrictions. CloudTrail event history can help identify the principal, API action, and resource involved in a failed request when the event is recorded. Do not respond to every denial by attaching AdministratorAccess. Correct the narrow missing permission and repeat the operation using the intended role."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-4-section-10",
        "title": "IAM Governance, Access Review, and Production Policy Testing",
        "content": "Production ECR permissions should be reviewed as part of the application's software supply-chain controls. Maintain separate roles for repository administration, image publishing, image retrieval, and human troubleshooting where practical. Use infrastructure code or policy-as-code checks to review changes before deployment, and identify wildcard resource statements that could be narrowed. Remove unused identities and rotate credentials according to the organization's security requirements. Test both expected success and expected denial cases so a policy does not grant more access than intended. Keep an auditable mapping between each pipeline or runtime component and the AWS role it assumes. During periodic reviews, confirm that repository permissions still match application ownership, deployment architecture, and cross-account dependencies."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Docker Authentication",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "How Docker Authentication Works with Amazon ECR",
        "content": "Docker authentication establishes a trusted relationship between the Docker client and an Amazon Elastic Container Registry endpoint before an image can be pushed or pulled from a private repository. Amazon ECR does not normally accept an ordinary AWS access key or secret access key directly as a Docker password. Instead, an authorized AWS identity requests a short-lived registry authorization token through the ECR GetAuthorizationToken API. The AWS CLI can obtain this token and pass it to Docker through the docker login command. The registry endpoint is specific to the AWS account and Region, so engineers must authenticate against the correct registry hostname. Authentication proves that the client has a valid credential, while IAM authorization determines which repository operations that identity may perform. A successful login therefore does not automatically guarantee permission to push or pull every image. In production, use IAM roles and temporary credentials instead of embedding permanent AWS keys in scripts, source repositories, or container images."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Understanding ECR Registry Endpoints and Regions",
        "content": "An Amazon ECR registry endpoint identifies the private registry belonging to an AWS account in a particular Region. Its common hostname format is ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com, where ACCOUNT_ID identifies the AWS account and REGION identifies the registry location. Docker must authenticate to the same endpoint that appears in the image reference being pushed or pulled. For example, logging in to an endpoint in one Region does not authenticate Docker to a different Region endpoint. A deployment pipeline that builds an image in one Region and publishes it to another must deliberately authenticate to each destination registry. Engineers should derive the account ID and Region from controlled pipeline variables or AWS identity information rather than manually copying an endpoint from an unrelated environment. Validate the active AWS account using aws sts get-caller-identity and confirm the configured Region before login. This prevents accidental publication to a development account, an incorrect production account, or an unintended geographic registry."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Obtaining a Short-Lived ECR Authorization Token",
        "content": "The AWS CLI command aws ecr get-login-password retrieves a password that Docker can use for ECR registry authentication. The command obtains an authorization token using the credentials and permissions of the currently active AWS identity. A typical login pipeline is aws ecr get-login-password --region us-east-1 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com. The username is the literal value AWS, while the password is supplied through standard input. The --password-stdin option is important because it avoids placing the password directly in command-line arguments, where it could be exposed through shell history or process inspection. The token is temporary and should be requested when the pipeline needs it rather than stored as a permanent secret. If the token request fails, investigate AWS credentials, the selected account, Region configuration, network connectivity, and IAM permission for ecr:GetAuthorizationToken before changing Docker configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "Authenticating Docker from a Developer Workstation",
        "content": "A developer workstation can authenticate to Amazon ECR using an AWS CLI profile configured with an approved identity. First, verify the active identity with aws sts get-caller-identity and confirm that the selected profile points to the intended account. Then select the correct Region and run aws ecr get-login-password --region REGION | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com. Docker should report that login succeeded when the credentials are accepted. The developer can then pull an authorized image or push an image to a repository for which the identity has the required permissions. Avoid placing passwords in shell commands, environment files committed to Git, or Dockerfiles. On shared workstations, be aware that Docker may retain authentication information in its local configuration. Developers should use approved credential helpers where available, protect their operating-system account, and remove stale authentication when access is no longer required. Workstation authentication should follow the same least-privilege and account-separation standards used by automated pipelines."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Using IAM Roles for EC2-Based Docker Authentication",
        "content": "An EC2 instance can authenticate to ECR without storing long-lived AWS access keys when it is associated with an appropriately configured IAM instance profile. The application or deployment process obtains temporary role credentials through the instance metadata service, and the AWS CLI uses those credentials when requesting an ECR authorization token. The role needs ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and the relevant repository permissions for the intended image operations. The instance must also be able to reach the ECR API and registry endpoints through its network configuration. A typical workflow verifies the instance identity, requests a login password for the target Region, and pipes that password into docker login. Restrict the instance role to the repositories and actions required by the workload. Do not attach broad administrator permissions merely to make a push or pull succeed. For private subnets, configure the required network path, such as appropriate VPC endpoints or controlled outbound connectivity. This approach reduces secret-management risk and allows AWS to rotate temporary credentials automatically."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Authenticating CI/CD Runners to Amazon ECR",
        "content": "A CI/CD runner should authenticate to ECR using a controlled machine identity rather than a developer's personal AWS credentials. On AWS-hosted runners, an attached IAM role can provide temporary credentials. For external CI platforms, prefer a supported federation mechanism such as AWS IAM role assumption through OpenID Connect when available. The pipeline should identify the target account and Region, obtain an ECR authorization password immediately before publishing, and pass it to Docker using --password-stdin. Separate build permissions from deployment permissions when the organization requires different trust boundaries. A build job may need permission to push to a staging repository, while a release job may be authorized to publish to production. Avoid printing tokens in job logs, enabling shell tracing around credential commands, or saving Docker configuration as an unrestricted build artifact. Configure runner cleanup so temporary credentials and authentication files do not remain available to later jobs on shared agents. Authentication failures should stop publication rather than trigger an unsafe fallback to hard-coded credentials."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Docker Credential Storage and Logout Practices",
        "content": "After a successful Docker login, the client stores registry authentication information in its Docker configuration, commonly located at ~/.docker/config.json on Linux-based systems or the corresponding Docker configuration directory on other operating systems. Depending on the credential-store configuration, the file may contain encoded authentication data or references to an external credential helper. Base64 encoding is not encryption, so a configuration file containing encoded credentials must still be protected. On shared build agents, persistent Docker configuration can unintentionally expose one job's registry access to another job. Use isolated runner accounts, approved credential helpers, restricted file permissions, and temporary workspaces. When the workflow finishes, docker logout ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com can remove the saved authentication entry. Logout is useful cleanup, but it does not replace short-lived credentials, runner isolation, or secure secret handling. Never copy a Docker configuration file containing authentication data into a container image or publish it as a build artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Cross-Account Docker Login and Repository Access",
        "content": "A Docker client accessing an ECR repository owned by another AWS account must authenticate to the destination account's registry endpoint and satisfy the destination repository's authorization rules. The caller may assume a role in the target account, or the repository owner may configure a repository policy that grants the required access to an external principal. The identity still needs permission to obtain an ECR authorization token, and the repository policy and identity permissions must permit the requested image operation. Cross-account access should use explicit account IDs, Region values, repository names, and approved trust relationships. Do not assume that a successful login to the caller's own registry grants access to another account. For automated deployments, document which account owns the repository and which role is allowed to publish or consume images. Validate both the assumed identity and the full image URI before executing Docker commands. This reduces the risk of publishing an image into the wrong account or using an unintended cross-account trust path."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Diagnosing Docker Authentication Failures",
        "content": "When Docker reports unauthorized, denied, or authentication-required errors while communicating with ECR, investigate the complete authentication path rather than repeatedly running docker login. First run aws sts get-caller-identity to establish which AWS principal is active. Confirm that the AWS Region and registry hostname match the repository's location. Request a fresh password with aws ecr get-login-password and authenticate using --password-stdin. If token retrieval fails, inspect AWS credential expiration, role assumption, network access to the ECR API, and ecr:GetAuthorizationToken permission. If login succeeds but a push or pull fails, inspect repository-level permissions such as ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, ecr:InitiateLayerUpload, ecr:UploadLayerPart, ecr:CompleteLayerUpload, ecr:PutImage, ecr:BatchGetImage, and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer as appropriate. Check repository policies for cross-account access and confirm that the image URI identifies the expected repository. Record sanitized error messages and request IDs, but never expose authorization tokens or secret values in diagnostic logs."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Tagging",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Docker Image Tags in Amazon ECR",
        "content": "A Docker image tag is a human-readable reference attached to an image that helps teams identify a particular build, release, or environment version. An ECR image reference commonly follows the structure ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/REPOSITORY:TAG. The repository identifies where the image is stored, while the tag identifies a named version within that repository. For example, payments-api:1.4.2 may represent an application release, whereas payments-api:staging may represent an environment-oriented reference. Tags are useful for organizing releases and integrating image publication into CI/CD workflows, but they should not be confused with the image's immutable content identity. A tag can be reassigned to a different image unless repository tag immutability is enabled. Production deployments should use a deliberate tagging strategy and retain the image digest for exact identification. Avoid relying only on vague names such as latest because they do not communicate which source revision or build produced the deployed artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "Designing a Consistent Image Tagging Strategy",
        "content": "A reliable ECR tagging strategy should make each published image traceable to its source code, build execution, and release decision. Common tag components include the application version, Git commit SHA, CI build number, and release channel. For example, an image might receive a semantic version tag such as 2.3.1 and a separate commit-specific tag such as git-a1b2c3d. The semantic version helps release teams understand compatibility and change history, while the commit tag identifies the source revision used for the build. Teams should define which tags are created for development, testing, staging, and production and decide which references are permitted to move. A commit-specific tag is generally more suitable for build traceability than a shared tag such as latest. Avoid generating inconsistent tag formats across services because automation becomes harder to validate. Document allowed characters, naming conventions, retention expectations, and whether tags are mutable or immutable before implementing the publishing pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Using Semantic Versioning for Container Images",
        "content": "Semantic Versioning provides a structured way to communicate application release compatibility through a MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH version such as 3.2.1. The major component indicates incompatible changes, the minor component represents backward-compatible functionality, and the patch component represents backward-compatible fixes. A CI pipeline can read the release version from a controlled source and use it as an ECR image tag. Teams may also publish pre-release identifiers such as 3.3.0-rc.1 for release candidates, provided their naming policy supports the chosen format. Semantic versions make release communication clearer, but they do not independently prove that an image was built from a particular commit or that its contents have not changed. Pair the release tag with a commit identifier and record the resulting image digest. Do not reuse an immutable production version for a different build. If a release needs correction, publish a new version according to the organization's release policy rather than silently replacing the artifact associated with an existing version."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Tagging Images with Git Commit and Build Identifiers",
        "content": "Commit-based and build-based tags improve the traceability of images produced by automated pipelines. A pipeline can obtain a short Git revision using git rev-parse --short HEAD and use a normalized value in a tag such as git-a1b2c3d. It can also attach a CI build identifier, for example build-1842, to distinguish separate executions. The full source revision should remain available in release metadata even if a shortened revision is used in the visible tag. Build identifiers are particularly useful when the same commit is built more than once with different build inputs, but they are meaningful only when linked to the CI system's records. Avoid putting credentials, personal information, or sensitive environment names in image tags. Ensure the tagging script handles empty Git metadata, detached checkouts, and invalid characters safely. A production release record should connect the source commit, pipeline run, image tags, digest, and deployment event so that engineers can reconstruct exactly what was released."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Applying Multiple Tags to One Docker Image",
        "content": "Docker supports assigning multiple tags to the same local image without rebuilding the image contents. After building an image with a unique local reference, a pipeline can use docker tag to add an ECR repository reference for a commit identifier and another reference for a release version. Both references can then be pushed to the registry. For example, a single built image may be tagged as payments-api:git-a1b2c3d and payments-api:2.3.1. This approach allows teams to provide both traceability and release-friendly naming while preserving one build artifact. However, each tag is a separate registry reference and can have its own lifecycle and mutability behavior. A pipeline should verify that all intended tags refer to the same image ID before pushing. Do not rebuild separately for each tag unless there is a deliberate reason, because different builds may produce different artifacts even when the source revision is unchanged. Record the digest returned by the registry after publication."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Publishing Versioned Tags to an ECR Repository",
        "content": "A versioned image publication workflow begins by selecting the target AWS account, Region, and ECR repository. The pipeline builds the image, assigns the required repository-qualified tags, authenticates to the registry, and pushes each approved tag. A typical command sequence includes docker build -t payments-api:2.3.1 ., docker tag payments-api:2.3.1 ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/payments-api:2.3.1, followed by docker push using the full ECR reference. If the pipeline also publishes a commit tag, it should apply that tag to the same local image before pushing. Confirm that the destination repository exists and that the publishing identity has the required upload and image publication permissions. Capture the command exit status and fail the job if any required push fails. After publication, query ECR image details to confirm that the expected tag exists and record its digest. Never report a release as successful merely because the local Docker build completed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Managing the Latest Tag Safely",
        "content": "The latest tag is a conventional image label, not a guarantee that the image contains the newest secure or production-approved application version. It can be reassigned to another image when repository settings permit tag mutation, and different teams may use the word latest to mean different things. A pipeline that automatically updates latest after every build can accidentally make an untested development image appear to be the current release. If an organization uses this tag, define exactly which pipeline stage is allowed to publish it and whether it represents the latest successful build, latest tested build, or latest approved production release. Prefer immutable commit or version tags for deployment automation. If a deployment consumes latest, record the resolved digest so that the actual deployed artifact remains identifiable. For repositories with tag immutability enabled, attempts to overwrite an existing latest tag will be rejected, so the release process must account for that policy. Treat latest as a convenience reference rather than the primary source of deployment identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "Verifying Tags and Image Metadata in ECR",
        "content": "After pushing an image, verify that ECR contains the expected repository, tag, and image digest. The AWS CLI command aws ecr describe-images --repository-name payments-api --region REGION can return image details including image tags, digest, and push timestamp. For a targeted check, use the image identifier parameter with the expected tag. Compare the returned digest with the digest captured from the push or subsequent registry inspection. A matching tag alone is not sufficient evidence that the intended artifact was published if the tag is mutable. Pipeline metadata should also record the source commit, build number, semantic version, and relevant build provenance. When an image is promoted between environments, verify that the destination reference resolves to the intended digest. These checks help identify missing pushes, incorrect repository names, stale tags, and accidental overwrites. Keep verification output free of secrets and use the evidence as part of the release record and deployment audit trail."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-6-section-9",
        "title": "Production Image Tagging Governance and Release Traceability",
        "content": "Production tagging governance defines how container artifacts are named, promoted, retained, and audited across the software delivery lifecycle. Establish a documented convention that combines human-readable release versions with immutable source or build identifiers, and capture the ECR digest for every approved release. Restrict which roles can publish or overwrite release tags, and enable tag immutability for repositories where existing release references must never be reassigned. Use separate policies for development and production repositories when their risk and release controls differ. Promotion between environments should ideally reuse the already-built image identified by its digest rather than rebuilding source code independently in each environment. Integrate image metadata, vulnerability results, approval records, and deployment history into the release evidence. Define how rollback selects a previously approved digest and how obsolete tags are handled through lifecycle policies. This governance makes it possible to answer which source revision produced a running container, who published it, which checks it passed, and exactly which artifact should be redeployed during recovery."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Push Workflow",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-1",
        "title": "End-to-End ECR Image Push Lifecycle",
        "content": "An Amazon ECR image push workflow transfers a locally available Docker image into a repository hosted by Amazon Elastic Container Registry. The process begins with selecting the correct AWS account, Region, repository, and image version. The build system creates the container image, applies a fully qualified ECR image reference, authenticates Docker using an ECR authorization token, and uploads the image layers and manifest. Docker communicates with the registry using the registry API and the required layer upload operations. ECR stores the image and makes it available to authorized consumers. A successful local build does not mean that the image has been published, so the pipeline must check the exit status of the push operation. After publication, verify the repository, tag, digest, and push timestamp using ECR image metadata. In a production release process, preserve the relationship between source commit, build execution, image digest, vulnerability results, and deployment record. This creates a traceable artifact lifecycle instead of treating image publication as an isolated shell command."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-2",
        "title": "Preparing the ECR Repository Before Publishing",
        "content": "Before pushing an image, confirm that the destination ECR repository exists in the intended AWS account and Region. Repository preparation includes choosing a consistent repository name, configuring encryption, deciding whether tags must be immutable, setting image scanning behavior, and defining lifecycle and access policies. A pipeline can check repository existence with aws ecr describe-repositories and create it through aws ecr create-repository when the deployment design permits automated provisioning. Production repositories should normally be provisioned through reviewed infrastructure as code so that security settings and policies remain consistent across environments. The publishing identity needs permission to obtain an authorization token and perform the repository upload operations. Confirm that the account ID, Region, repository name, and image URI are derived from trusted configuration. A repository name mismatch can result in publishing an artifact to an unintended location or a repository-not-found error. Validate these details before building or uploading large images, and fail early when the target repository is unavailable or incorrectly configured."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-3",
        "title": "Building a Container Image for ECR Publication",
        "content": "The image build step converts an application source tree and Dockerfile into a container image that can be stored in ECR. The pipeline should select a known source revision, use the intended build context, and apply controlled build arguments and dependency versions. A typical command is docker build -t payments-api:build-1842 ., where the local tag identifies the application and build execution. Use a reviewed Dockerfile, avoid copying secrets into image layers, and use a .dockerignore file to exclude unnecessary files such as local credentials, temporary build output, and version-control metadata. Multi-stage builds can separate compilation dependencies from the final runtime image and reduce the published image size. Record the build result and stop the pipeline if Docker returns a non-zero exit code. Where reproducibility matters, pin base images by digest and control external dependency versions. After building, inspect the local image ID and metadata so that the exact artifact can be tagged and published without accidentally rebuilding a different image later in the release process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-4",
        "title": "Constructing the Fully Qualified ECR Image URI",
        "content": "A Docker image must use the correct registry-qualified reference when it is pushed to Amazon ECR. The general format is ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/REPOSITORY:TAG. The account ID identifies the AWS account that owns the registry, the Region selects the registry location, the repository identifies the image collection, and the tag identifies the intended version. For example, 123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/payments-api:2.3.1 is a complete image reference when those values correspond to the real destination. A pipeline should construct the reference from validated variables and avoid embedding production account details in scripts that are reused across environments. Confirm that the selected AWS identity belongs to the expected account or is authorized to publish cross-account. Also verify that the repository exists in the same Region represented in the hostname. A malformed hostname, wrong account ID, repository spelling error, or Region mismatch can cause login or push failures. Print the non-secret destination URI in build logs so reviewers can verify where the artifact is being published."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-5",
        "title": "Authenticating Docker Before an ECR Push",
        "content": "Docker must authenticate to the target ECR registry before it can publish an image to a private repository. The pipeline should first obtain AWS credentials from an approved identity, such as an EC2 instance role, an assumed role, or a federated CI identity. It can then request a temporary registry password with aws ecr get-login-password --region REGION and pass the output to docker login using --username AWS and --password-stdin. The registry hostname supplied to docker login must match the hostname in the image reference. The identity requires ecr:GetAuthorizationToken for token retrieval and the necessary repository permissions for uploading image layers and publishing the image manifest. Do not store the temporary password in source code, shell history, build artifacts, or unmasked pipeline output. If authentication fails, verify the caller identity, credential expiration, Region, endpoint, and network connectivity before changing repository policies. Keep authentication and publication steps separate in logs so that teams can distinguish credential failures from repository authorization failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-6",
        "title": "Applying Release and Commit Tags Before Pushing",
        "content": "Before uploading the image, assign tags that make the artifact traceable and useful to release operators. A pipeline may apply a semantic version such as 2.3.1 and a commit-specific tag such as git-a1b2c3d to the same locally built image. Docker can create these additional references using docker tag without rebuilding the image. The destination references must include the complete ECR registry and repository path. Validate the source image exists locally and confirm that each target tag points to the same image ID. Use a consistent naming policy across services and avoid using latest as the only production identifier. If the ECR repository enforces tag immutability, a previously published tag cannot be overwritten, so the pipeline should generate unique identifiers or intentionally fail when a release tag already exists. Record the source revision, build number, and resulting tags in release metadata. Applying tags before the push ensures that the intended references are uploaded as part of the same controlled publication process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-7",
        "title": "Uploading Image Layers and Manifests to ECR",
        "content": "The docker push command initiates publication of the tagged image to the selected ECR repository. Docker compares local image layers with the registry and uploads layers that are not already available there. ECR handles the layer upload workflow and stores the image manifest that references the required content. A typical command is docker push ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/payments-api:2.3.1. Large images may take time to transfer depending on layer size, network bandwidth, and registry behavior. A pipeline should wait for the command to finish and capture its exit status rather than treating intermediate progress messages as success. Reusing stable base layers and ordering Dockerfile instructions effectively can improve caching and reduce unnecessary transfer. If an upload is interrupted, inspect the final repository state before retrying and confirm that the intended tag has not been published with unexpected content. Do not suppress errors or continue into deployment when a required image push fails."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-8",
        "title": "Verifying the Published Image and Digest",
        "content": "After a push completes, verify that the expected image tag is present in the correct ECR repository. The AWS CLI command aws ecr describe-images --repository-name payments-api --region REGION can return image details such as image digest, tags, and push timestamp. A digest identifies the image manifest content and provides a stronger artifact reference than a mutable tag. Compare the registry digest with the digest captured by the build or publication workflow whenever that evidence is available. Confirm that the repository and account are correct and that all required tags appear. If the tag is missing, investigate whether the push targeted a different Region, account, or repository. If the tag exists but the digest differs from the expected artifact, stop promotion and investigate possible tag reuse or an incorrect build. Store the digest with the release metadata and use it for downstream deployment where exact artifact identity is required. Verification should be an explicit pipeline gate rather than a manual assumption."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-9",
        "title": "Integrating ECR Push into a CI/CD Release Pipeline",
        "content": "A production CI/CD pipeline should make image publication a controlled stage between successful application validation and deployment promotion. A typical sequence checks out a specific source revision, runs tests, builds the container, scans the image, authenticates to ECR, tags the image, pushes it, verifies the resulting digest, and records release metadata. Each stage should have a clear success condition and should stop downstream work when a required check fails. Use an IAM role or federated identity with narrowly scoped permissions instead of permanent access keys. Separate development and production destinations when different approval requirements apply. Protect release tags and ensure that only approved pipeline identities can publish production artifacts. Store the image URI and digest as pipeline outputs so later deployment stages consume the exact artifact that passed validation. Avoid rebuilding the application image during promotion because a second build may produce different contents. This design provides consistent provenance and supports audit, rollback, and incident investigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-7-section-10",
        "title": "Troubleshooting Failed ECR Image Push Operations",
        "content": "A failed ECR push can result from missing repository permissions, an expired login token, an incorrect registry endpoint, a nonexistent repository, a tag immutability conflict, or network connectivity problems. Begin by capturing the exact Docker error and identifying the target image URI. Run aws sts get-caller-identity to confirm the active principal and verify the configured Region. Request a fresh ECR password and authenticate to the exact registry hostname. If Docker reports access denied during upload, inspect permissions for ecr:InitiateLayerUpload, ecr:UploadLayerPart, ecr:CompleteLayerUpload, ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, and ecr:PutImage as appropriate, along with any repository policy. If the tag already exists in an immutable repository, publish a new unique tag instead of trying to overwrite it. For network errors, check DNS resolution, proxy settings, firewall rules, and VPC endpoint configuration. Inspect the repository afterward to determine whether any tag was actually published. Never bypass a production security policy simply to make a failed push succeed."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Pull Workflow",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding the ECR Image Pull Lifecycle",
        "content": "An Amazon ECR image pull retrieves a container image from a private registry so that Docker or a container runtime can use it locally. The consumer identifies the repository and image tag or digest, authenticates to the correct registry, requests the image manifest, and downloads the layers that are not already available in its local cache. ECR checks the caller's permissions and provides access to the requested image content. The image reference includes the AWS account, Region, repository, and tag or digest. Pulling by tag is convenient, but a mutable tag can later point to different image content. Pulling by digest identifies a specific image manifest and is therefore preferable when a deployment must use an exact approved artifact. The client must have network connectivity to the registry and permission to retrieve the image. A successful pull proves that the client retrieved the image, but production systems should also validate image provenance, security scan results, and deployment policy before running the container."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-2",
        "title": "Preparing an Environment for Private ECR Image Pulls",
        "content": "Before pulling an image, verify the target account, Region, registry hostname, repository name, and requested image identifier. Confirm that the machine or workload has a valid AWS identity and that the identity is permitted to retrieve the repository content. On an EC2 instance, an instance profile can provide temporary role credentials. In Kubernetes environments, node-level or workload-specific image retrieval configuration must be designed according to the cluster platform and supported authentication mechanism. Confirm that the environment can resolve and reach the ECR API and registry endpoints. Private subnets may require appropriate VPC endpoints or another approved network path. The Docker daemon or container runtime must also be installed, running, and configured for the intended architecture. These preparation checks prevent teams from confusing infrastructure problems with registry authorization failures. For production environments, validate the image reference through deployment configuration and ensure that the requested version or digest is approved for the target environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "Authenticating Docker for a Private ECR Pull",
        "content": "A private ECR repository requires the consumer to authenticate before retrieving an image. The AWS identity first obtains a temporary registry authorization password using aws ecr get-login-password --region REGION. The password is passed to Docker with docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com. The client must authenticate to the same registry hostname used in the image reference. The identity needs ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and the repository retrieval permissions required for the image, including ecr:BatchGetImage and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer as applicable. For cross-account pulls, the repository owner must also authorize the external identity through the appropriate repository policy or role arrangement. Avoid storing registry passwords in deployment manifests or permanent configuration files. In automated environments, use temporary credentials and supported platform integrations. If authentication fails, verify the active identity, Region, token freshness, and destination endpoint before investigating image tags or local Docker cache state."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Pulling an ECR Image by Tag",
        "content": "To pull an image by tag, use the complete ECR image reference followed by docker pull. For example, docker pull ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/payments-api:2.3.1 retrieves the image associated with the 2.3.1 tag at the time of the request. Before executing the command, authenticate to the same registry and confirm that the identity can read the repository. Docker downloads the manifest and required layers, reusing layers already present locally when possible. A successful command indicates that the requested reference was retrieved, but it does not establish that the tag is immutable or that the image is approved for production. Record the resolved digest when deployment traceability matters. If the tag does not exist, inspect ECR image details and confirm the repository name, account, Region, and exact tag spelling. Avoid assuming that latest points to the same content across environments or at different times. Use controlled release tags and deployment approval checks for production workloads."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Pulling Images by Digest for Exact Artifact Identity",
        "content": "An ECR image digest is a content-derived identifier for an image manifest and provides a precise way to select an artifact. Instead of relying on a potentially mutable tag, a consumer can use a reference in the form ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/payments-api@sha256:DIGEST. Docker can pull the image by this digest after authenticating to the registry and receiving the required repository permissions. Digest-based deployment helps ensure that staging and production use the same published artifact, even if human-readable tags change later. A release pipeline should capture the digest after publication, store it in release metadata, and pass it to deployment systems as an approved input. Confirm that the digest belongs to the intended repository and that the image has passed the required security and release checks. A digest identifies content, not business approval, so teams should still maintain provenance and promotion records. Using digest references is particularly valuable for reproducible deployments, incident investigation, and reliable rollback."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-6",
        "title": "Inspecting Local Images After an ECR Pull",
        "content": "After pulling an image, inspect the local Docker image inventory and metadata to confirm what was retrieved. The command docker images can show repository names, tags, image IDs, and sizes. The command docker image inspect can provide detailed configuration such as architecture, operating-system information, environment metadata, entrypoint, command, and image layers. Compare the local reference with the expected ECR repository and release identifier. When exact artifact identity is required, inspect the registry digest using ECR image metadata and compare it with the release record. Avoid exposing sensitive environment values if image inspection output is copied into shared logs. An image being present locally does not guarantee that it is safe or suitable to run; verify its source, scan results, runtime configuration, and approval status. If an unexpected image appears, stop deployment and determine whether the wrong tag, account, Region, or registry was used."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-7",
        "title": "Using ECR Pulls in EC2 and Kubernetes Workloads",
        "content": "EC2 instances and Kubernetes worker nodes commonly retrieve private container images from ECR as part of application deployment. An EC2-based process can use an instance role and Docker authentication to pull an image directly. In Amazon EKS, the node or supported image-pull integration must be configured with the appropriate permissions and network access so the container runtime can retrieve the image. The exact mechanism depends on the cluster authentication mode, node configuration, and workload architecture. Ensure that the required repository read permissions are attached to the correct identity and that private networking can reach the registry endpoints. Kubernetes image references should use the intended account, Region, repository, and preferably a release digest for production workloads. When pods report image pull errors, inspect pod events and node-level runtime logs, then validate the registry URI and identity permissions. Do not solve a node authentication issue by granting broad permissions to every application workload."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-8",
        "title": "Optimizing Image Pull Performance and Network Reliability",
        "content": "Image pull performance depends on image size, layer reuse, network bandwidth, registry location, and the number of nodes retrieving the artifact simultaneously. Smaller runtime images reduce transfer time and storage consumption, while stable Dockerfile layer ordering improves cache reuse. Place compute workloads and ECR repositories in Regions and network paths that meet latency, availability, and data-transfer requirements. For private subnets, configure the appropriate ECR API and Docker registry connectivity, including required S3 access for image layer downloads where applicable. Avoid repeatedly pulling unnecessarily large images during every deployment. Use controlled caching and node lifecycle practices without weakening image freshness or security requirements. When many nodes start together, plan for deployment concurrency and observe pull latency and failure rates. Monitor network throughput, DNS resolution, endpoint health, and runtime logs when pulls are slow. Optimization should preserve digest verification and security scanning rather than relying on unverified cached artifacts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-8-section-9",
        "title": "Troubleshooting ECR Image Pull Failures",
        "content": "Image pull failures can be caused by an invalid image URI, missing repository permissions, expired authentication, absent tags, network restrictions, or incompatible image architecture. Start by capturing the exact error from Docker or the container platform. Confirm the active AWS identity, registry account, Region, repository, and tag or digest. Authenticate to the correct registry and verify that the identity has ecr:GetAuthorizationToken, ecr:BatchGetImage, and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer permissions as required. For Kubernetes, inspect pod events and node runtime logs to distinguish authorization failures from image-not-found or transport errors. Check repository policies for cross-account access and confirm the requested image exists with aws ecr describe-images. Investigate DNS, firewall, proxy, VPC endpoint, and S3 layer-download connectivity when the failure is network-related. Verify that the image architecture is compatible with the target runtime. Do not switch to an untrusted public image as a quick workaround for a production pull failure."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Immutable Image Tags",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding ECR Image Tag Immutability",
        "content": "Image tag immutability is a repository-level control in Amazon Elastic Container Registry that prevents an existing image tag from being overwritten by a different image. In a mutable repository, a publisher can reuse a tag such as 2.4.0 or production and make it refer to newly published image content. This behavior can create uncertainty because two deployments using the same tag at different times may receive different artifacts. When tag immutability is enabled, ECR rejects attempts to push an image using a tag that already exists in the repository. The original tag-to-image association remains protected from accidental replacement through normal image publication. This control is valuable for release tags, audited production artifacts, and environments where deployments must be reproducible. Immutability does not prevent users with deletion permissions from deleting images, and it does not replace IAM authorization, repository policies, lifecycle governance, or digest verification. Teams must design their tagging strategy so every new build receives a suitable unique tag."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-2",
        "title": "Configuring Immutable Tags During Repository Creation",
        "content": "A new ECR repository can be configured with tag immutability when it is created. Using the AWS CLI, an administrator can run aws ecr create-repository with the repository name, Region, and image-tag-mutability IMMUTABLE setting. The repository can also be provisioned through infrastructure as code so the setting is reviewed, version-controlled, and consistently applied across environments. Before enabling the setting, define how developers and pipelines will create unique tags for every image publication. A pipeline that repeatedly pushes the same tag will fail after the first successful publication. Repository creation should also consider encryption, image scanning configuration, access policies, and lifecycle rules because immutability is only one part of repository governance. After provisioning, verify the effective setting with aws ecr describe-repositories and inspect the returned imageTagMutability property. Avoid assuming that a console configuration or infrastructure deployment succeeded without checking the actual repository configuration. Record the setting as part of the repository's operational baseline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-3",
        "title": "Changing Existing Repository Tag Mutability",
        "content": "ECR supports changing a repository's tag mutability configuration when operational requirements change. An administrator can use aws ecr put-image-tag-mutability to update the repository setting, subject to the identity's permissions. Before changing a production repository from mutable to immutable, inspect existing tags and update publishing pipelines so they no longer depend on overwriting a shared release reference. Changing from immutable to mutable can allow future pushes to replace existing tag references, so the change should require explicit approval and a documented reason. Some ECR configurations support exclusion filters for selected tags, allowing a repository to enforce immutability generally while permitting specifically named tags to remain mutable. Such exceptions should be narrow, reviewed, and tested against the actual repository configuration. After the change, query the repository again and verify the returned setting and filters. Treat mutability changes as security-relevant configuration changes, not as routine troubleshooting actions performed without review."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-4",
        "title": "Designing Unique Tags for Immutable Repositories",
        "content": "An immutable repository requires a tag strategy that avoids reusing an existing tag for a different image. Common identifiers include semantic release versions, full or shortened Git commit revisions, CI build numbers, and controlled release identifiers. For example, a pipeline might publish payments-api:2.5.0 and payments-api:git-a1b2c3d-build-1842. The release version communicates application compatibility, while the commit and build identifiers improve traceability. A build pipeline should generate tags deterministically from trusted metadata and validate that the required tag is available before attempting publication. Avoid relying on a permanently reused tag such as latest unless an explicitly configured mutability exception supports that workflow. If a tag already exists in an immutable repository, the pipeline should stop and report the conflict rather than deleting or replacing the existing artifact. Document the naming convention, permitted characters, uniqueness rules, and relationship between release tags and image digests so teams can publish consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-5",
        "title": "Handling Image Push Conflicts in Immutable Repositories",
        "content": "When a Docker push attempts to publish an image using an existing immutable tag, ECR rejects the operation because the tag is already associated with an image. The pipeline should treat this as a meaningful release conflict rather than a generic network failure. First, capture the exact error and identify the repository and tag involved. Use aws ecr describe-images with the repository name and image tag to inspect the existing image's digest and push metadata. Compare the existing digest with the artifact the pipeline intended to publish. If the existing image is the correct release artifact, the pipeline may be able to reuse it after verifying provenance. If the new build is different, generate a new unique tag according to the release policy. Do not delete the existing image simply to make the push succeed, especially in a production repository. Such deletion can break deployments, audits, or rollback procedures. A well-designed pipeline should detect tag collisions early and provide a clear failure message that explains the conflicting tag and the safe next step."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-6",
        "title": "Using Mutable Tag Exclusions with Immutable Repositories",
        "content": "Some ECR repository configurations support IMMUTABLE_WITH_EXCLUSION and MUTABLE_WITH_EXCLUSION modes, which apply a general tag mutability behavior while defining selected tag patterns as exceptions. This can be useful when release tags must remain immutable but an operational alias such as latest or a controlled development reference needs to move. Exclusion filters should be designed narrowly and should not accidentally match release tags. For example, a team might allow a specific development tag pattern to be mutable while protecting semantic release and commit-specific tags. Configure the repository through the supported AWS CLI or infrastructure tooling, then inspect the effective imageTagMutability and exclusion filter configuration. Test both matching and non-matching tags in a non-production repository before applying the policy to production. Remember that a mutable exception increases the need for digest recording and deployment verification because the tag can identify different image content over time. Document the owner and purpose of each exception."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-7",
        "title": "Protecting Production Releases with IAM and Repository Policies",
        "content": "Tag immutability protects existing tag references from being overwritten, but it does not determine who may publish, delete, or retrieve images. Production repository security should combine immutable release tags with least-privilege IAM policies and, where appropriate, ECR repository policies. Restrict ecr:PutImage and layer upload permissions to approved publishing roles. Restrict image deletion permissions such as ecr:BatchDeleteImage to a small administrative or lifecycle-management identity. Separate build identities from release promotion identities when the delivery process requires additional approval. Cross-account publishers should receive only the repository access needed for their role. Use CloudTrail and deployment records to investigate who performed repository operations and when. A user who can delete an image may still disrupt availability even when its tag is immutable, so protect deletion separately. Review policy changes, role trust relationships, and exception filters as part of the production security review. Immutability is strongest when combined with controlled publication and tightly governed deletion."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Integrating Immutable Tags into CI/CD Pipelines",
        "content": "A CI/CD pipeline using immutable ECR tags should generate a unique artifact reference for each approved build and avoid reusing previously published release identifiers. The pipeline can derive a commit tag from Git metadata and combine it with a CI execution number when repeated builds of the same commit are possible. It should build once, assign all intended tags to that image, authenticate to ECR, and push the references. If an immutable tag collision occurs, stop the release and inspect the existing digest instead of attempting deletion or overwrite. After publication, capture the registry digest and expose both the tag and digest as pipeline outputs. Downstream deployment stages should consume the approved digest where exact artifact identity is required. Include tag policy checks in pull requests or pipeline validation so teams discover naming problems before a production release. Keep the publishing role narrowly scoped and prevent untrusted pull-request jobs from publishing production images. This makes immutability an effective part of release engineering rather than a setting that causes unexpected build failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-9-section-9",
        "title": "Operational Governance and Troubleshooting for Immutable Tags",
        "content": "Operational governance for immutable tags should define how releases are named, promoted, retained, and recovered. Maintain a documented policy that identifies protected release tags, permitted mutable exceptions, authorized publishing roles, and image deletion responsibilities. Monitor pipeline failures for tag conflicts and distinguish them from authentication, authorization, and network errors. When a conflict occurs, inspect the existing tag and digest, compare them with the expected artifact, and decide whether to reuse the verified image or publish a new unique tag. Do not weaken repository mutability merely to bypass a failed release. Lifecycle policies should be reviewed carefully so that they do not remove images still referenced by active deployments or required for rollback. Test repository settings and publishing workflows in a non-production environment before changing production controls. During an incident, use the digest and release metadata to identify the correct artifact rather than assuming that a familiar tag still represents the intended version. Review configuration drift periodically and keep repository policy evidence available for audits."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Image Digests",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Docker and ECR Image Digests",
        "content": "An image digest is a content-derived identifier used to identify a specific container image manifest. In Amazon ECR, image digests are commonly displayed as SHA-256 values prefixed with sha256:. Unlike a mutable image tag, a digest identifies the manifest content and provides a precise reference to the published artifact. An ECR image can be referenced using the format ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/REPOSITORY@sha256:DIGEST. If a tag is moved to another image, the digest of the original image remains a distinct content identifier. This makes digests valuable for reproducible deployments, release verification, audit evidence, and rollback. A digest does not itself prove that an image is safe, trusted, or approved; it only identifies content. Teams must still validate provenance, vulnerability results, and release authorization. Understanding the difference between a tag and a digest is essential when designing reliable container delivery systems that must deploy exactly the artifact that passed testing."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "How SHA-256 Image Digests Identify Content",
        "content": "ECR image digests use a cryptographic hash to identify the image manifest content. A SHA-256 digest is represented by a fixed-length hexadecimal value associated with the manifest bytes. When the manifest content differs, its digest will normally differ as well. This allows a consumer to identify an image independently of a human-readable tag. Docker and registry clients use digest references to retrieve the specific manifest identified by that value. The digest should be copied exactly from trusted registry metadata rather than manually shortened or reconstructed. A manifest digest identifies the manifest, which in turn references image configuration and layers; it should not be confused with a single layer digest or local image ID. Multi-platform images can involve an image index or manifest list that references platform-specific manifests. Engineers should understand which digest their deployment tooling records and ensure that the selected platform receives the intended image. Use registry-provided digest metadata as the authoritative reference for release operations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Retrieving Image Digests from Amazon ECR",
        "content": "The AWS CLI can retrieve image digest information from an ECR repository using aws ecr describe-images. For example, aws ecr describe-images --repository-name payments-api --image-ids imageTag=2.5.0 --region REGION requests metadata for the specified tag. The response can include imageDigest, imageTags, imagePushedAt, imageSizeInBytes, and scan-related metadata depending on the available response fields and configuration. A pipeline can extract the digest and store it as a release output for later deployment stages. If the query returns no image details, confirm that the repository, Region, account, and tag are correct and that the caller has permission to describe images. Do not assume a successful command returned the intended release without checking the response. For production use, compare the retrieved digest with the expected build or publication record. Preserve the full digest value in deployment configuration and audit records so that later troubleshooting does not depend on an ambiguous tag."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Pulling ECR Images by Digest Instead of Tag",
        "content": "A consumer can pull an ECR image by digest using docker pull followed by the repository reference and an @sha256 digest suffix. For example, docker pull ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/payments-api@sha256:DIGEST requests the specific manifest identified by the supplied digest. The Docker client must first authenticate to the registry and have permission to retrieve the repository content. Digest-based pulls are useful when a deployment must use the exact artifact that passed validation, because a mutable tag may point to a different image later. The digest should be supplied by a trusted release process, deployment manifest, or approved artifact record. Validate that it belongs to the expected repository and that the image has passed the required security and release checks. After the pull, record the resolved image reference in deployment evidence. Do not copy incomplete or truncated digest strings into production configuration. This approach reduces ambiguity when the same tag name is reused across development, staging, and production workflows."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "Comparing Tags, Digests, and Local Docker Image IDs",
        "content": "Tags, registry digests, and local Docker image IDs are related but represent different forms of image identification. A tag is a human-readable reference that can be reassigned when repository settings allow it. An ECR digest identifies registry manifest content and is suitable for exact remote artifact references. A local Docker image ID identifies the local image configuration and is useful when inspecting images on a build machine. These identifiers should not be treated as interchangeable. A pipeline can build an image, tag it with a release name, push it to ECR, and then capture the registry digest returned by ECR metadata. The local image ID can help verify which locally built image was tagged, while the registry digest confirms the published manifest reference. Multi-platform builds may add further distinctions between an image index digest and platform-specific manifest digests. Record the identifiers needed by the build, release, and deployment systems, and document which one is authoritative for each operation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Using Image Digests for Reproducible Deployments",
        "content": "Reproducible container deployment requires the deployment system to select the same artifact that was validated and approved during the release process. A mutable tag can change its target between testing and production, creating a risk that different environments run different image contents. Capturing the ECR digest after publication allows the release pipeline to pass an exact image reference to downstream deployment systems. Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, ECS task definitions, or other deployment configuration can use a digest-qualified image reference where supported. The release record should connect the digest to the source commit, build execution, scan results, approval, and deployment event. When promoting an image from staging to production, reuse the existing artifact rather than rebuilding it. This reduces variation caused by dependency updates, build-time differences, or changing base images. Digest-based references do not replace runtime security controls, but they provide a stable artifact identity that supports repeatable deployment and reliable investigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "Digest Verification, Provenance, and Supply Chain Security",
        "content": "Digest verification is an important part of container supply chain security because it allows deployment systems to compare the requested artifact with trusted release metadata. A pipeline should obtain the digest from ECR after publication and associate it with the source revision, build identity, build environment, scan results, and approval record. Before deployment, verify that the digest matches the approved release record and that the repository belongs to the expected AWS account and Region. Additional supply chain controls may include signed attestations, provenance statements, software bills of materials, and image signature verification through supported tooling. A digest alone cannot prove who built an image or whether the build process was trustworthy. It only identifies the content being referenced. Combine digest verification with protected CI workflows, restricted publishing permissions, vulnerability management, and deployment policy enforcement. During an incident, use the recorded digest to determine exactly which artifact was deployed and compare it with the image currently available in the registry."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Using Digests for Rollback and Production Incident Recovery",
        "content": "A reliable rollback process should identify a previously approved image by digest rather than relying only on a historical tag name. Before each production deployment, store the deployed image digest with the release identifier, source revision, deployment timestamp, and environment. If a release causes an incident, operators can select the digest associated with the last known-good deployment and update the workload to use that exact image reference. Confirm that the image still exists in ECR and that lifecycle policies have not removed it. The rollback identity must have permission to retrieve the artifact, and the deployment system must be able to reach the registry. Validate application compatibility, database migration constraints, and configuration changes before rolling back the container alone. A previous image may not be safe to run if the surrounding infrastructure or data schema has changed. After recovery, record the incident timeline and the digest used for rollback. Retaining verified release metadata makes recovery faster and reduces uncertainty during production incidents."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Lifecycle Policies",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Amazon ECR Lifecycle Policies",
        "content": "Amazon ECR lifecycle policies automate the expiration of images that are no longer required in a repository. Container registries can accumulate many image versions as development builds, release candidates, and production releases are published. Without a retention strategy, storage usage and image-management complexity can grow continuously. A lifecycle policy evaluates repository images against configured rules and identifies images that meet expiration criteria. Rules can use image tag patterns, tag status, image age, and image count to determine which images are eligible for expiration. The policy is defined as JSON and can contain multiple prioritized rules. When a policy is applied, ECR evaluates the rules and performs expiration actions asynchronously rather than deleting every matching image immediately. Lifecycle policies should be designed around deployment, audit, and rollback requirements. They are not a substitute for backup planning or release governance. Before enabling expiration, identify which images must remain available for active workloads, incident recovery, compliance, and long-term release traceability."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Designing Lifecycle Policy Rules and Priorities",
        "content": "An ECR lifecycle policy contains rules that define which images are selected and when they become eligible for expiration. Each rule includes a priority, a selection criterion, and an action such as expire. The priority determines the order in which rules are evaluated, and each rule should have a unique priority value within the policy. Selection criteria can identify tagged or untagged images and can use tag prefixes or patterns supported by ECR lifecycle policy syntax. Age-based criteria can expire images after a defined number of days, while count-based criteria can retain a specified number of recent images. Rules should be designed so that their matching behavior is understandable and does not unintentionally remove protected releases. For example, a development repository may retain a limited number of recent build images, while a production repository may preserve approved release tags for a longer period. Validate the complete JSON structure and review rule interactions before applying the policy to a repository containing important artifacts."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "Managing Untagged Image Retention",
        "content": "Untagged images can remain in an ECR repository after tags are removed, because removing a tag does not necessarily remove the underlying image manifest and layers. These untagged artifacts may result from repeated builds, tag updates, image promotion workflows, or cleanup operations. Over time, they can consume registry storage and make repository inventory harder to manage. A lifecycle policy can select untagged images and expire them after a suitable period or according to a count-based strategy where supported. Before configuring expiration, confirm that the untagged images are not required by an active deployment or a recovery process. Some release workflows deliberately remove temporary tags while retaining content for a short transition period, so immediate cleanup may be unsafe. Use aws ecr describe-images to inspect image metadata and identify untagged artifacts before changing retention rules. Apply the policy first in a non-production repository and verify the expected lifecycle preview. A controlled retention period allows teams to remove abandoned artifacts without disrupting release operations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Retaining Recent Images with Count-Based Rules",
        "content": "Count-based lifecycle rules retain a defined number of recent images that match a selection criterion and make older matching images eligible for expiration. This approach is useful for repositories that receive frequent CI builds and need to preserve a predictable number of recent artifacts. For example, a development repository might retain the latest 30 images with a particular build tag pattern, while older matching images become eligible for cleanup. The correct count depends on build frequency, testing duration, deployment rollback needs, and operational retention requirements. A count that is too small can remove artifacts needed by a long-running test or delayed release. A count that is too large can increase storage usage without providing meaningful recovery value. Separate development and production retention strategies where their requirements differ. Verify how rules interact with other selection criteria and priorities, and inspect the lifecycle preview before applying changes. Count-based retention should be based on actual release and incident-response needs rather than an arbitrary number."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Applying Age-Based Image Expiration Rules",
        "content": "Age-based lifecycle rules identify images according to how long they have existed in the repository and make matching images eligible for expiration after the configured period. This can help remove short-lived test builds, temporary integration artifacts, and outdated development images. The retention duration should reflect the time needed for testing, release approval, operational investigation, and rollback. A development image might be eligible for cleanup after a shorter interval than a production release artifact. Do not assume that image age alone indicates whether an artifact is safe to delete. A release deployed several months ago may still be needed for recovery or audit evidence. Use tag selection criteria to distinguish temporary build images from protected release versions. Review the image push timestamps and lifecycle preview before applying the policy. Keep important production artifacts in a retention class that meets organizational requirements, and ensure that lifecycle automation cannot remove the only available copy of a required release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Using Tag Prefixes and Patterns for Targeted Cleanup",
        "content": "Tag-based lifecycle selection allows a repository policy to target image groups using naming conventions. Teams can use distinct prefixes for development builds, pull-request builds, release candidates, and production versions. A policy can then apply different retention behavior to each group. For example, temporary build references might use a ci- prefix, while approved releases use a v- or release- convention. ECR lifecycle policy selection supports tag-based matching features defined by its policy syntax, so engineers should verify whether the intended filter uses tag prefixes or supported tag patterns rather than assuming arbitrary regular expressions are accepted. Naming conventions must be consistent across all pipelines; otherwise, some images may escape cleanup or protected images may match an unintended rule. Test filters against real repository inventory and inspect the lifecycle preview. Avoid using broad matching rules in production without understanding every tag they select. A precise tag taxonomy makes automated retention safer and simplifies repository administration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "Previewing and Testing Lifecycle Policy Actions",
        "content": "Before applying an ECR lifecycle policy, use the supported lifecycle policy preview operation to understand which images the rules are expected to expire. The AWS CLI provides aws ecr start-lifecycle-policy-preview and aws ecr get-lifecycle-policy-preview operations for this workflow. The preview helps administrators inspect the effects of rule selection and priority without immediately relying on an expiration result. Review image tags, digests, timestamps, and rule matches, paying particular attention to production releases and artifacts used by active environments. A safe rollout begins in a development repository with representative images and realistic tag naming. After the preview is understood, apply the policy using aws ecr put-lifecycle-policy and monitor the resulting lifecycle activity. Remember that expiration actions are asynchronous, so the repository may not change immediately after the policy is applied. Keep a copy of the approved policy JSON and its review evidence so future changes can be compared against the previous retention behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-8",
        "title": "Protecting Production Images and Rollback Artifacts",
        "content": "Production lifecycle policies must preserve the images required to operate the application and recover from failed releases. Before creating expiration rules, identify the current production digest, previous known-good releases, long-running deployment versions, and artifacts retained for audit or compliance. Avoid relying only on a small recent-image count when deployments may remain active for long periods or rollback requirements extend beyond the most recent releases. Use separate repositories or well-defined tag groups for development and production artifacts when this simplifies retention governance. Restrict who can modify lifecycle policies and review policy changes through infrastructure as code or a controlled approval process. Ensure that an image required for rollback cannot be removed merely because it has an old push timestamp. Where long-term preservation is required, evaluate an approved archival or replication strategy and verify that the retained copy can be retrieved. Test rollback procedures using the actual digest and confirm that the artifact remains accessible before considering a retention policy production-ready."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-11-section-9",
        "title": "Monitoring, Troubleshooting, and Maintaining Lifecycle Policies",
        "content": "Lifecycle policy maintenance includes checking policy configuration, reviewing preview results, monitoring repository storage, and investigating unexpected image expiration. Use aws ecr get-lifecycle-policy and aws ecr describe-images to inspect the active policy and repository inventory. If an image disappears unexpectedly, identify the matching lifecycle rule, its priority, selection criteria, and the image metadata that made it eligible. Check whether a tag was removed or changed before the policy evaluation. Lifecycle actions can be asynchronous, so allow for processing time and do not assume that a policy failure occurred simply because an image is still visible immediately after application. If no images are expiring, confirm that the rules match actual tags and that the policy has been successfully applied. If too many images are selected, stop and revise the policy before relying on it in production. Maintain version-controlled JSON, change approvals, and periodic reviews of retention requirements as application release frequency and rollback expectations evolve."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Encryption and Access Control",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Encryption at Rest in Amazon ECR",
        "content": "Amazon ECR encrypts image data at rest so that stored container artifacts are protected from unauthorized access to underlying storage. Encryption at rest is an important control for repositories containing application code, proprietary dependencies, and other sensitive build artifacts. ECR supports server-side encryption using AWS-managed encryption keys by default and can also use customer-managed AWS Key Management Service keys when the repository is configured for that purpose. Encryption protects stored data, but it does not replace repository authentication, IAM authorization, network security, or application-level secret management. A user who has permission to retrieve an image can still access its contents through an authorized pull operation. Repository owners should therefore combine encryption with least-privilege access policies and controlled publishing identities. Select encryption settings when designing the repository and understand the operational consequences of changing key configuration. Document the encryption approach for each environment and verify that the repository configuration matches organizational security and compliance requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-2",
        "title": "Using AWS-Managed Keys for ECR Encryption",
        "content": "ECR repositories use AWS-managed encryption by default unless a different supported encryption configuration is selected. AWS-managed keys reduce the administrative work associated with creating and maintaining a customer-managed KMS key. AWS handles key infrastructure and routine service integration, while repository access remains controlled through IAM and ECR policies. This option can be suitable when an organization does not require independent control over key policies, custom key rotation settings, or detailed key administration. Teams should still understand which encryption mode is active and ensure that it meets internal security requirements. Encryption at rest does not make an image public or private by itself; repository policies and IAM permissions determine who can retrieve image content. Verify repository configuration through the AWS console or supported ECR APIs and include the encryption setting in infrastructure documentation. If a compliance requirement demands customer-controlled key administration, evaluate a customer-managed KMS key before creating the repository rather than assuming the default configuration provides those controls."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-3",
        "title": "Configuring Customer-Managed AWS KMS Keys",
        "content": "A customer-managed AWS KMS key gives an organization additional control over encryption key administration for an ECR repository. The key can be created and managed through AWS KMS, and the repository can be configured to use it when supported by the selected ECR repository settings. Administrators must consider key policy permissions, IAM permissions, key lifecycle, rotation, deletion protection, and service integration. The identity creating or managing the repository needs the relevant permissions to configure encryption, while the ECR service must be able to use the key according to the supported service authorization model. Restrict key administration to approved roles and avoid broad key policies that grant unnecessary access. Before using a customer-managed key in production, test repository creation, image push, image pull, and recovery behavior. Key disablement or deletion can make encrypted data inaccessible, so operational safeguards are essential. Document the key ARN, owning account, Region, key administrators, and recovery contacts as part of the repository's security record."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-4",
        "title": "Understanding KMS Key Policies and Permissions",
        "content": "AWS KMS key policies define which principals can administer or use a customer-managed key, and they are central to controlling access to encrypted ECR repository data. IAM permissions alone do not automatically grant access when the key policy does not authorize the required principal or delegate permissions appropriately. Key administrators should be separated from ordinary application publishers and image consumers. Review key policy statements for actions needed to manage the key and for service usage permitted by the ECR integration. Avoid granting unrestricted kms:* access to broad groups or every CI runner. A repository publisher should receive the minimum permissions required to push images, while a key administrator should manage key configuration under a separate operational role. Test the full workflow using the intended identities because an overly restrictive key policy may cause repository operations to fail. Monitor key policy changes and maintain an approved recovery process for accidental key disablement or permission removal."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-5",
        "title": "Controlling ECR Access with IAM Identity Policies",
        "content": "IAM identity policies determine which ECR API actions an AWS principal can perform. A developer, CI runner, EC2 role, or deployment service should receive only the permissions required for its responsibility. A publisher may need ecr:GetAuthorizationToken and repository upload actions such as ecr:InitiateLayerUpload, ecr:UploadLayerPart, ecr:CompleteLayerUpload, ecr:BatchCheckLayerAvailability, and ecr:PutImage. A consumer typically needs ecr:GetAuthorizationToken, ecr:BatchGetImage, and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer. Administrative actions such as creating repositories, changing policies, or deleting images should be assigned to separate trusted roles. Scope repository-level actions to specific repository ARNs where supported, while recognizing that authorization-token retrieval is an account-level action with different resource-scoping behavior. Validate permissions using the actual workflow and review CloudTrail events when access is denied. Avoid solving a narrow permission failure by attaching broad administrator policies to the entire pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-6",
        "title": "Designing ECR Repository Policies for Resource Access",
        "content": "An ECR repository policy is a resource-based policy attached to a repository and can grant access to principals that need to interact with that repository. Repository policies are particularly important for cross-account access, where a principal from another AWS account needs to push or pull images. A policy should identify the trusted principal, permitted ECR actions, and intended repository resource. Grant only the required operations; a consumer should not receive image publication or deletion rights unless those duties are genuinely required. Repository policies work together with identity policies and other AWS authorization controls, so a resource policy should not be treated as a replacement for reviewing the caller's IAM permissions. Use aws ecr set-repository-policy to apply an approved policy and aws ecr get-repository-policy to inspect the current configuration. Validate account IDs, role ARNs, and action names carefully. Review policy changes through a controlled process and monitor for unexpected external principals or overly broad access statements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-7",
        "title": "Implementing Least-Privilege Push and Pull Roles",
        "content": "Least-privilege design separates the permissions needed to publish an image from those needed to consume it. A build role may require ECR token retrieval and upload operations for one or more approved repositories. A deployment role or worker-node identity may need only the permissions required to retrieve images. A repository administrator can have separate permissions to create repositories, configure encryption, update lifecycle policies, and manage access policies. This separation reduces the impact of a compromised build runner or workload. Use IAM roles with temporary credentials rather than distributing permanent access keys. Scope repository actions to the smallest practical set of repository resources and review cross-account trust relationships. Test each role using the exact CI or runtime workflow because missing one required action can cause a push or pull failure. Maintain permission documentation and remove obsolete grants when services or pipelines are retired. Least privilege should be validated continuously as repositories, deployment platforms, and release workflows change."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-8",
        "title": "Securing Private Registry Access and Network Paths",
        "content": "ECR access security includes both identity authorization and the network path used by clients to reach the registry. Private workloads may use VPC endpoints to reach ECR APIs and registry services without relying on unrestricted public internet access. Image layer retrieval also requires the supported data path used by ECR, so network design must account for the relevant S3 connectivity where applicable. Security groups, endpoint policies, route tables, DNS resolution, and proxy configuration can all affect whether a client can authenticate and download image content. A successful AWS API request does not prove that the Docker registry endpoint or image layer download path is reachable. Restrict endpoint policies to approved principals and repositories where supported, and ensure that network controls do not unintentionally block required service communication. Test both authentication and actual image push or pull from the intended private subnet. Document the network architecture and monitor connectivity failures separately from IAM authorization errors."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "Auditing ECR Encryption and Access Changes",
        "content": "Auditing ECR security requires visibility into repository configuration, image access, IAM policy changes, repository policy updates, and KMS key administration. AWS CloudTrail records supported AWS API activity and can help investigators determine which principal performed an operation and when it occurred. Review events associated with repository creation, repository policy changes, image publication, image deletion, and relevant KMS administration. Use AWS Config or an equivalent configuration governance process where available to detect deviations from approved repository settings. Periodically inspect repository encryption configuration, image tag mutability, lifecycle policies, and access policies. Investigate unexpected changes to external principals, broad IAM grants, or key permissions. Retain audit records according to organizational requirements and ensure that access to the audit destination is restricted. Logging does not prevent unauthorized access by itself, but it supports detection, investigation, and accountability when combined with least-privilege authorization and security monitoring."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-09-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "Production Security Governance and Encryption Troubleshooting",
        "content": "Production ECR governance should define who can create repositories, configure encryption, administer KMS keys, publish images, retrieve artifacts, and delete content. When an encrypted repository operation fails, identify whether the problem is an IAM denial, repository policy restriction, KMS key policy issue, disabled key, incorrect Region, or network failure. Inspect the active AWS identity with aws sts get-caller-identity and review the relevant repository and key configuration before modifying permissions. Check CloudTrail events for authorization failures and request details, but do not expose secret material in incident records. If a customer-managed key is disabled or inaccessible, follow the approved key recovery procedure and involve the designated key administrators. Do not replace a restrictive production policy with unrestricted access as a troubleshooting shortcut. Maintain tested recovery contacts, documented key ownership, policy backups, and configuration reviews. Validate security controls in a non-production environment before changing production repositories, and record every approved exception with an owner and expiration or review date."
      }
    ]
  },
